ription:"**All** scenarios affect the indicator Annual Caloric Demand of Food Including Waste by altering demand across eight key food categories. The first four categories in the legend represent animal-based foods, while the last four represent plant-based foods.",graph_fff1_title:"Daily Caloric Demand per Capita [Food]",graph_fff2_description:"This variable represents the total annual calorie production of the global agricultural system in response to changing food demand. Here, the supply of food is aggregated as plant-based and animal-based products as it helps to emphasise two fundamentally different production systems of crop and livestock farming.",graph_fff2_title:"Annual Caloric Supply [Plant, Animal]",graph_fff3_description:"Annual caloric demand of food including waste across 4 food categories of animal-based food.",graph_fff3_title:"Annual Caloric Demand [Animal-based]",graph_fff4_description:"Annual caloric demand of food including waste across 4 food categories of plant-based food.",graph_fff4_title:"Annual Caloric Demand [Plant-based]",graph_fff5_description:"**All** scenarios affect the indicator Annual Caloric Demand of Food Including Waste by altering demand across eight key food categories. The first four categories in the legend represent animal-based foods, while the last four represent plant-based foods.",graph_fff5_title:"Effective Caloric Demand [Food]",graph_fff6_description:"Total production of animal-derived foods (e.g., beef, pork, poultry). Typically requires more land, water, and feed, contributing to higher environmental impacts.",graph_fff6_title:"Animal-based Food Production",graph_fff7_description:"Total production of plant-derived foods (e.g., cereals, legumes, vegetables, fruits). Generally more land- and resource-efficient than animal-based production.",graph_fff7_title:"Plant-based Food Production",graph_fu129sdsd_description:"Movement of nitrogen through the environment (e.g., leaching, volatilization, runoff). Helps identify major loss pathways and their environmental impacts.",graph_fu129sdsd_title:"Nitrogen Flows [Source]",graph_fu12ds9ssdd_description:"Pathways of phosphorus movement and loss (e.g., surface runoff, crop uptake). Key for managing efficiency and minimizing pollution.",graph_fu12ds9ssdd_title:"Phosphorus Flows [Source]",graph_fu16_description:"Total nitrogen present in the agricultural system, including soil, crops, and external inputs. Reflects nutrient availability, potential surplus, and pollution risks.",graph_fu16_title:"Nitrogen",graph_fu17_description:"Total phosphorus in the system, including soil stock and added inputs. Important for assessing long-term fertility and risk of overaccumulation.",graph_fu17_title:"Phosphorus",graph_fu1dsfdsfsdf_description:"Crop output per unit of cultivated land. Measures agricultural productivity and land-use efficiency.",graph_fu1dsfdsfsdf_title:"Cropland Yield",graph_fua_description:"Total synthetic nitrogen applied to agricultural land, excluding organic sources. Synthetic nitrogen is crucial for crop growth, but its excessive use can lead to water pollution, soil degradation, and greenhouse gas emissions.",graph_fua_title:"Nitrogen Application in Agriculture",graph_fub_description:"Total synthetic phosphorus applied to agricultural land, excluding organic phosphorus. Essential for plant development, but over-application increases the risk of water pollution and algal blooms.",graph_fub_title:"Phosphorus Application in Agriculture",graph_fuc_description:"Nitrogen used to produce specific food types (e.g., cereals, legumes, animal feed). Animal-based food production often requires more nitrogen, contributing to higher environmental harm through runoff and emissions.",graph_fuc_title:"Nitrogen Application in Agriculture [Food]",graph_fud_description:"Phosphorus applied to grow various food types. Animal-based and feed crops typically demand more phosphorus, intensifying risks of nutrient runoff and water eutrophication.",graph_fud_title:"Phosphorus in Agriculture Application [Food]",graph_hvy_description:"Source: FAOSTAT. Protein supply quantity for Animal Products [S2941] and Vegetal Products [S2903]. Units: g/capita/day. Year: 1961-2013",graph_hvy_title:"(FAOSTAT) Protein Supply per Capita",graph_hvz_description:"Source: FAOSTAT. Fat supply quantity for Animal Products [S2941] and Vegetal Products [S2903]. Units: g/capita/day. Year: 1961-2013",graph_hvz_title:"(FAOSTAT) Fat Supply per Capita",graph_lu0_description:"Proportion of total land allocated to different uses (e.g., cropland, pasture, forest). Captures landscape structure and resource allocation.",graph_lu0_title:"Land Use Composition [Land Use]",graph_lu4_description:"Estimated agricultural land area required based on food demand and production systems. Highlights land pressure under current land use types.",graph_lu4_title:"Agriculture Land Needed [Land Use]",graph_lu999939_description:"Average agricultural output per unit of cropland area. A key indicator of land productivity and farming efficiency.",graph_lu999939_title:"Cropland Yield",graph_lu99999_description:"Annual rate of forest area lost due to land conversion, primarily for agriculture or development. High rates indicate environmental pressure and biodiversity loss.",graph_lu99999_title:"Deforestation Rate",graph_lu9999_description:"Total land area allocated to agriculture and forest uses. Reflects how land is distributed between productive and natural ecosystems.",graph_lu9999_title:"Land Use [Agriculture, Forest Land]",graph_lu999_description:"Land area required to produce one calorie of food. Lower values indicate more land-efficient food systems.",graph_lu999_title:"Land Use Per Calorie",graph_lu99_description:"Estimated land demand broken down by specific land types (e.g., arable, pasture). Useful for evaluating suitability and trade-offs.",graph_lu99_title:"Agriculture Land Needed [Land Type]",graph_sum0_title:"Land | Agricultural Land Needed",graph_sum0a_title